---
import "../styles/paper.css";
import "katex/dist/katex.min.css";
import katex from "katex";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Two players claim cells of an n by n grid; the first to own four corners of a square wins. The first player wins exactly when n is at least 5, by a machine-checked strategy. Play against it."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="masthead">
      <h1>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</h1>
      <p class="byline">Sean Reid · August 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="abstract">
      <strong>Abstract.</strong> Two players alternately claim cells of an
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid; the first to hold all four corners
      of an axis-aligned square wins. We show the first player has a winning strategy
      precisely when <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />, and that all smaller
      boards are drawn under optimal play. The strategy is explicit, and the
      searches that verify it are formalized in Lean 4, so each verified board
      size is a theorem rather than a program run.
    </p>

    <figure class="game">
      <sag-board n="5"></sag-board>
      <figcaption>
        Figure 1. Play the game: you are O, the bot opens as P1 and replays the
        strategy below move for move. Change n to explore; below 5 × 5 neither
        side can force a win.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The game</h2>
    <p>
      Played on the cells of an <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid, P1 (X)
      moving first. A winning set is any
      <span
        set:html={k("\\{(r,c),\\,(r,c{+}s),\\,(r{+}s,c),\\,(r{+}s,c{+}s)\\}")}
      /> with <span set:html={k("s \\ge 1")} />: the four corners of an
      axis-aligned square, from 2 × 2 blocks up. A full board with no completed
      square is a draw. Tilted squares do not count. Both players chase the same
      goal, so a player who can no longer win can still spoil; that is why small
      boards end drawn rather than lost.
    </p>

    <div class="theorem">
      <span class="label">Theorem 1.</span> The first player has a winning strategy
      in the square achievement game on the
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board if and only if
      <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />.
    </div>

    <h2>History</h2>
    <p>
      Games of this shape are achievement games, introduced by Harary: both
      players build toward the same target, first to finish wins. Erickson asked
      what happens for the axis-aligned square on an
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid, and the question is recorded on the
      Open Problem Garden. Two ends of the answer were known. Jenrich reported computer
      searches giving draws on 3 × 3 and 4 × 4 and a first-player win on 5 × 5. At
      the other end, Bacher and Eliahou proved that every two-coloring of a 15 × 15
      grid contains a monochromatic square, so no game on a board of size 15 or more
      can end in a draw; a classical strategy-stealing argument then hands the win
      to the first player, without exhibiting a single move of a strategy. In between,
      from 6 × 6 through 14 × 14, the outcome was open. That is the range we close,
      and the proof is a strategy you can watch: the bot in Figure 1 is playing it.
    </p>

    <h2>The strategy</h2>
    <p>
      Everything rests on two reflexes, checked before anything else on every
      turn: complete one of your own squares if you can; otherwise, if the
      opponent is one cell from completing theirs, block it. What remains is
      choosing the quiet moves, and those come from a fixed lookup table. The
      strategy opens at (2,2), answers the opponent's first reply with a tabled
      second move, and the first two replies together select a plan: a short
      list of cells played in order.
    </p>
    <p>
      Plans are forcing. Each plan move puts three corners of some square in
      place, so the opponent must block at once or lose, until one move creates
      two threats and no single block answers both. The opponent's forced blocks
      pile up too, and sometimes complete three corners of their own square; the
      block reflex answers those counter-threats, and the search that generated
      the table only accepted plans that keep the initiative through such
      exchanges.
    </p>
    <p>
      One table serves every board from 6 × 6 up, for two reasons. First, its
      plans never leave the 6 × 6 corner region of the board, so every square
      the strategy builds exists on every board containing that region. Second,
      a reply far from the corner region can join a square through two of the
      strategy's stones only from the region's own rows and columns, so far
      replies are looked up by geometric class rather than by exact position.
      The 5 × 5 board is the odd one out: it is too cramped for pure forcing,
      and exhaustive analysis shows that no all-forcing table wins there. Its
      table comes instead from a full solution of the 5 × 5 game and includes
      quiet moves that fork on the opponent's reply.
    </p>

    <h2>Why believe a computer search</h2>
    <p>
      For each board size, an exhaustive search plays the strategy against every
      reply sequence; at 9 × 9 that is over three million positions. This game
      already shows why such searches deserve suspicion: the first published
      search for 5 × 5 contained an error and was later corrected. The search
      here is implemented three times independently, in Python, in Lean, and in
      the TypeScript bot on this page, and all three agree on every size tested.
      Only one of the three carries mathematical weight: the Lean checker comes
      with a machine-checked soundness theorem, stating that if the checker
      returns true then a winning strategy tree exists for the abstract game. A
      program's output is just a bit; the soundness theorem is what turns that
      bit into mathematics. The strategy tables enter the development as plain
      data, so nothing about how they were found needs to be trusted.
    </p>

    <h2>Toward every board at once</h2>
    <p>
      For boards of size 15 and up the win is indirect: no draw is possible, and
      the second player cannot have a winning strategy, so the first player
      does. That argument names no move. We conjecture that the table strategy
      wins on every board of size 6 and up, which would replace the indirection
      with one explicit strategy for all of them. The obstacle is concrete. A
      patient opponent can plant stones far from the corner region, whose
      squares exist only on very large boards, and harvest forced exchanges from
      them. The reflexes answer each such threat, but every answer costs a
      tempo, and a table tuned on one finite board can be led off script on a
      larger one. We have not yet ruled that out for every board at once; the
      sizes through 14 × 14 are settled unconditionally.
    </p>

    <h2>Formalization and downloads</h2>
    <p>
      The proof is formalized in Lean 4: the game semantics, an executable
      verifier with a machine-checked soundness theorem, and the strategy tables
      as certificates; the assembly for arbitrary board size is in progress.
      Both artifacts are built from this repository on every deploy:
      <a href="downloads/square-achievement-paper.pdf">the paper (PDF)</a> and
      <a href="downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz"
        >the Lean development (tar.gz)</a
      >.
    </p>

    <footer>
      Code MIT · text CC BY 4.0 · built from the square-achievement repository.
    </footer>

    <script>
      import "../game/board";
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
